  "text": "**Solutions 2 and 3 have the correct final answer, and they agree.** Solution 1 is wrong because its sign analysis excludes the range $\\beta\\in(s-1,0]$.\n\n**Check of the sign of the constant.** For $u = c\\,x_n^\\beta$, $(-\\Delta)^s x_n^\\beta = \\lambda(s,\\beta)\\,x_n^{\\beta-2s}$ on $x_n>0$. The constant $\\lambda$ vanishes at $\\beta=s$ and $\\beta=s-1$, since $x_+^s$ and $x_+^{s-1}$ are $s$-harmonic on the half-line. At $\\beta=0$, $(-\\Delta)^s\\mathbf{1}_{\\{x_n>0\\}}(x) = C\\int_{y<0}|x-y|^{-1-2s}\\,dy > 0$, so $\\lambda(s,0)>0$. Hence $\\lambda>0$ on $(s-1,s)$, not just on $(0,s)$. Solution 1's claim that $\\lambda<0$ for $\\beta<0$ contradicts this.\n\n**Interval for $\\alpha$.** Matching exponents gives $\\beta=\\frac{\\alpha+2s}{1+\\gamma}$, and existence requires $\\beta\\in(s-1,s)$. Solving for $\\alpha$:\n- $A=(s-1)(1+\\gamma)-2s$\n- $B=s(\\gamma-1)$\n\n**Sum.**\n$$A+B = (sγ - s - 1 - γ) + (sγ - s) = 2sγ - 2s - γ - 1.$$\n\nSolution 3's form $(1+\\gamma)(2s-1)-4s$ expands to the same expression. Both reduce to $(-2,\\gamma-1)$ at $s=1$, and the $s=\\tfrac12$ check in Solution 2 is consistent with $\\beta\\cot(\\pi\\beta)$.\n\nThe sign argument is my own check rather than a citation of the classification result. I did not verify the exact interval against the published theorem, so this rests on the scaling argument and the sign analysis.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
